For most mounting scenarios, the wall mount provides a quick, simple, and flexible means of mounting your device. The installation should be done in a few simple steps: 1. Leave protective plastic sticker on camera bubble


2. Use template to determine mounting hole locations before screwing in the mount plate. Peel backing from mount template to stick on wall. Slide the camera onto the wall mount.
<PIC:Manual33_0>
{{image description: Black-and-white line illustration of a dome security camera body with a raised bubble or dome cover. Dashed outlines inside the dome suggest the internal lens assembly position. No text labels are present.}}




3. Screw the mounting plate onto the wall in pre-determined locations. Use template holes marked with the letter "A" for standard wall mounting.
<PIC:Manual33_1>
{{image description: Mount plate installation template drawing with many labeled hole positions and the Cisco logo. The central text lists mount types: A Wall, B Meraki MA-MNT-MV-1 Accessory, C 4-inch Square Junction Box (US), D Single-Gang Outlet Box, E European Outlet Box, F Meraki MA-MNT-MV-3 Accessory, G Below Drop Ceiling (T-Rail), H 4-inch Round Junction Box (US), and I 3.5-inch Round Junction Box (US). Hole labels such as A,C; B; C,D,E,H,I; D; H; E,I; F; G; and C,F appear around the template.}}




4. Connect PoE cable to camera. For cords that will exit the top of the camera, loop the cable inside the camera as shown.
<PIC:Manual33_2>
{{image description: Exploded mounting diagram showing a mount plate installation template on the left, a metal mounting plate on the right, and four screws aligned with holes by dotted guide lines. The plate includes tabs, slots, and circular holes; the template includes the Cisco logo and small mount-type labels.}}




5. Slide camera over top of mount plate and slide down into mount plate hooks. Secure with safety screw.
<PIC:Manual33_3>
{{image description: Close-up line drawing of the top of a round camera mount with a thick black cable looped through a rectangular opening or channel. The cable exits upward from the camera body area; no readable labels are present.}}




6. Turn bubble counter clockwise to unlock. Hinge bubble off of body to remove.
<PIC:Manual33_4>
{{image description: Two alternative mounting diagrams separated by the word 'or'. Each shows a mounting plate beside the dome camera body, a downward arrow indicating the camera sliding onto the plate, and a screw aligned with the top hole of the camera. The lower option also shows a cable emerging from the top opening.}}




7. Pinch near thumb screws and pull straight away from the camera to remove lens guard.
<PIC:Manual33_5>
{{image description: Two side-view line drawings of the dome camera bubble. Large curved arrows show the bubble being twisted or hinged open; the right view exposes the internal camera assembly behind the partially opened dome. No text labels are present.}}




8. Aim the lens. Look through the camera on the Dashboard to fine-tune the picture. The camera sensor and lens unit can be physically tilted through a range of 65 degrees, rotated through a range of 350 degrees, and panned through a range of 350 degrees. The image can only be rotated by 180 degrees in software and no other adjustments can be made. Zoom and focus can be adjusted remotely and cannot be adjusted physically on the camera.
<PIC:Manual33_6>
{{image description: Line drawing of the camera with the dome removed, exposing the lens module and internal adjustment hardware. A bold arrow points from the open camera toward a separate curved lens guard piece, indicating removal of the guard.}}




9. Replace lens guard and bubble. Turn bubble clockwise to lock.
<PIC:Manual33_7>
{{image description: Instructional aiming diagram with four rows. The center drawings show camera lens module adjustments labeled TILT (65 degrees MAX), TWIST (350 degrees MAX), and ROTATE (350 degrees MAX) with arrows. The left column shows room and camera viewing examples, and the right column shows corresponding view-framing examples such as windows, stairs, a tilted doorway, and a centered double door.}}




10. Remove protective plastic sticker. Check LED function. Use the Dashboard to adjust camera focus and configure other settings.
<PIC:Manual33_8>
{{image description: Reassembly diagram showing the open camera body on the left, a lens guard in the middle, and the dome bubble on the right. Bold arrows point the dome and guard back toward the camera, and a curved arrow on the dome indicates rotation.}}




<PIC:Manual33_9>
{{image description: Final camera diagram showing the assembled dome camera on the left and a protective sticker or film piece being pulled away to the right with a bold arrow. A dotted callout points to a small indicator on the camera and the readable note says, 'Check LED function. Refer to table below.'}}

